    {
      "game": "EA Sports FC 27",
      "game_image": "https://gaming-cdn.com/images/products/23585/450x258/ea-sports-fc-27-xbox-series-x-s-xbox-one-microsoft-store-cover.jpg?v=1791781200",
      "game_url": "https://www.instant-gaming.com/en/23585-buy-ea-sports-fc-27-xbox-series-x-s-xbox-one-microsoft-store/",
      "user": "gamer-c3b4e84",
      "avatar": "https://gaming-cdn.com/themes/igv2/images/avatar2.svg",
      "sentiment": "like",
      "content": "I thought this was a good way to buy fc27 for myself and it worked out to be an amazing idea as it helped me to save a bit of money and also play a game I wanted too. The price was perfect for me and the code for the game came instantly which was really impressive meaning I could download straight away"
    },
    {
      "game": "The Blood of Dawnwalker",
      "game_image": "https://gaming-cdn.com/images/products/15628/450x258/the-blood-of-dawnwalker-pc-steam-cover.jpg?v=1791781200",
      "game_url": "https://www.instant-gaming.com/en/15628-buy-the-blood-of-dawnwalker-pc-steam/",
      "user": "Said",
      "avatar": "https://gaming-cdn.com/images/avatars/16123731-1767222796.jpg",
      "sentiment": "like",
      "content": "Great RPG, but too much Witcher3/Ubisoft\n\n\n\nFinished the game after around 35 hours, and it's definitely a good game. I really enjoyed the first 20-25 hours. The world is great, the atmosphere is dark, and some of the stories, especially the side quests, are genuinely fantastic. I also liked the choices and different ways to approach quests. The whole \"save your family\" motivation is pretty overused, but that didn't really bother me.\n\n\n\nWhat increasingly annoyed me, though, was the old Witcher 3 and Ubisoft formula. Question marks everywhere, quest markers, and even freaking viewpoints. Instead of discovering the world yourself, you constantly check the map and work your way through a checklist. Why?\n\n\n\nI think this game would've been perfect as a sort of semi hardcore RPG. I don't want to spend hours desperately searching for some unmarked NPC, but I also don't want the map showing me every point of interest before I've even explored the area. Let me discover things naturally and give me markers once I've actually found them. And yes, I know you can turn those markers off, but that doesn't really solve the problem. I generally assume the developers' default settings are how the game is intended to be played.\n\n\n\nI actually really enjoyed the combat at first. But the longer I played, the more noticeable the clunkiness became. And the game kept throwing waves of enemies at me, which absolutely killed the pacing, especially towards the end. I eventually switched from Hard to Easy, not because the fights were too difficult, but because I just wanted to get them over with and continue the story.\n\n\n\nI don't think the game needed less content or a smaller world. It just needed fewer checklists and more trust in the player's curiosity. The content is already good. There's no need to constantly advertise all of it on the map. What's the point of designing a game around different choices, outcomes and replayability if you're simultaneously afraid of players missing something? Isn't discovering things you missed the first time part of what makes a second playthrough interesting?\n\n\n\nDespite all that, it's a really good RPG and a strong debut from Rebel Wolves. I'll probably never play it again because I've already experienced pretty much everything relevant to me, and the gameplay issues would only bother me more on a second playthrough.\n\n\n\nStill, I'm definitely looking forward to the sequel. I hope the studio stays independent and has the courage to move further away from the old Witcher formula next time. They've already done a lot differently and introduced some genuinely interesting ideas, which makes it even more frustrating that they also carried over some of the exact same design choices that even Witcher 3 fans criticized back then. I really hope they improve on those instead of repeating them again."
    },
